  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nIR currently shows some increasing clouds pushing in from the\nsouthwest out of northern Utah and eastern Idaho west of the\nDivide. Some light shower activity will push in as well by\nsunrise Tuesday morning <20 percent in coverage that will\ncontinue through much of the day. Minimal QPF and no impacts are\nexpected with this subtle mid level shortwave propagating up \nthe L/W pattern. The main upper level low is currently off the \nnorthern California coast deepening with increasing divergence \naloft and southward movement. This will continue to push south \nTuesday, beginning its eastward migration come Wednesday that \nwill be the next system to affect the northern Rockies and much \nof the CWA Thursday and beyond. The main finger of the PFJ \ncontinue to be well north stretching from the Pacific northwest \nand into southern Canada, keeping the aforementioned low cutoff \nfrom the meridional pattern for the the entire country.\n\nA slight warming trend continues through Thursday ahead of this\nsystem, remaining about 5 to 10 degrees above average for this\ntime of October but changes are incoming for Friday and beyond\nover the weekend and into the following work week. The low is\ntrending to become more of an open and more progressive wave as\nit nears western Wyoming for Thursday. Ahead of this system, \nexpect a tightening gradient about 5 to 7 mb difference across \nthe state. Southwest winds will uptick up to 40 to 50 mph \ngusts, possible higher instances for the wind corridor of \nSweetwater to Natrona Counties. Higher terrain of South Pass to \nCasper Mountain could push 60 mph with steering flow winds \nmixing to the surface under daytime heating of the afternoon. \n\nAs a cold front drops through late Thursday and into Friday,\nexpect cooling temperatures dropping freezing levels down to\n8-9kft. This will allow for mountain snow to develop late\nThursday stretching into Friday and the weekend. Model trends\ncontinue to be fairly consistent on at least advisory level snow\ntotals along and west of the Divide for the higher terrain \nareas. With the lowering freezing levels, expect impacts to \nmountain passes to include Teton and Togwotee for at least a \ncouple inches. As the system progresses further northeast, \nexpect the Bighorns to get the brunt of the snowfall that will \nlikely see impacts to the Granite and Powder River passes. Stay \ntuned for updated forecasts and updates to any needed headlines \nin the coming days. \n\nValley rain will be more prevalent west of the Divide and basins\nto the east later Friday. Much of the area should at least see a\nchance for a bit or a cold rain, higher QPF amounts to the west\ndue to the southwest to western flow of this system. Minimal\naccumulations expected east of the Bighorns being on the \nleeward side of this system. A second wave of the dipping jet \nstream come late weekend into early next week will reinforce the\ncooler, more fall like air with more mountain snow and valley \nrain to be expected if the longer term model solutions come into\nfruition. Being a week out, time will tell but the weather \npattern looks to be changing and more active for the latter part\nof October as we inch closer to winter.",
